<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Malacca</h1><p class="st-meta">1511 CE – 1641 CE · capital Malacca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's fortified trading base on the Strait of Malacca, seized in 1511 and held for 130 years until the Dutch took it in 1641.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured the city of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate</a> in August 1511, aiming to control the spice trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the Far East. He built the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a> on the hill by the river, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> became a key link in Portugal's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-india">Estado da India</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Portuguese captain governed the city, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> from 1557. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> remained a cosmopolitan port, though many Muslim merchants moved to rival ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> preached there in 1545. Intermarriage produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kristang">Kristang</a>, a Portuguese-Eurasian community whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> language still survives.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city withstood repeated sieges by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a>. In 1640 to 1641 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, besieged it for five months, and it fell in January 1641. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">Porta de Santiago</a> gate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kristang">Kristang</a> community remain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-malacca.jpg" alt="Portuguese Malacca" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: myself, based on ancient national symbol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(1640).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1511 CE – 1641 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Malacca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Afonso de Albuquerque</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malacca (Portuguese)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1576&amp;focus=portuguese-malacca" aria-label="Portuguese Malacca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7523 2096 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Malacca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7843 2352L7839 2352M7843 2352L4773 1044M7843 2352L7839 2352"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7839 2352h0M4773 1044h0M7839 2352h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7843 2352h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7843 2352h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1576&amp;focus=portuguese-malacca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
